  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Wireless Survey", "technique": "T1422", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-12 09:14:06 CEST, a wpa_supplicant / NetworkManager wireless scan was initiated on UBNT-FLOOR2-OPS (agent 10.44.21.37) enumerating all visible SSIDs in the corporate airspace and listing existing NetworkManager connection profiles. Wazuh logged this through NetworkManager log decoding — rule.description carries the scan event plus the visible-network list. The scan output included multiple corporate SSIDs (CorpWiFi, CorpGuest, CorpIoT) alongside several non-corporate SSIDs. This is the host-side equivalent of a wireless airspace survey: inventory the RF environment before introducing a new network profile.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh decoder fired on NetworkManager 'device wifi list' / wpa_supplicant scan-results events from agent.name=UBNT-FLOOR2-OPS. rule.groups contains 'networkmanager' + 'wireless'. The event lists visible SSIDs in rule.description.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:14:06+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Allowlist Comparison", "technique": "T1422", "what_happened": "At 09:17:40 CEST, the host compared the scanned SSID list against the corporate allowlist (CorpWiFi, CorpGuest, CorpIoT, CorpManagement, Corp-Visitor) and logged each non-allowlisted SSID for context. Wazuh captured these as a sequence of low-level NetworkManager informational events. This is the operator filtering step — separating known-good corporate SSIDs from the unknown ones that an unauthorized profile could be attached to.", "chain_reason": "NetworkManager informational log lines from agent.name=UBNT-FLOOR2-OPS, ~3.5 min after step 1, enumerating SSID strings not present on the corporate allowlist. rule.groups contains 'networkmanager'.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:17:40+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence / Initial Access — Wi-Fi Networks", "technique": "T1200", "what_happened": "At 09:24:55 CEST, a NEW NetworkManager connection profile file 'rogue-ap-20260512_091406.nmconnection' was created in /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/ on UBNT-FLOOR2-OPS. Wazuh FIM (syscheck) is configured to watch that directory and fired rule 92953 'New WiFi network profile created outside policy' [T1200] at critical. The profile defines an unauthorized SSID 'FreeWiFi_20260512', autoconnect=true, key-mgmt=none (open, no encryption). A new .nmconnection file outside the change-management process, defining an SSID not on the corporate allowlist, with autoconnect enabled, is the persistence mechanism — the host will silently rejoin this unauthorized network on every boot.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM rule 92953 fired: syscheck event 'added' on path /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/rogue-ap-20260512_091406.nmconnection, agent.name=UBNT-FLOOR2-OPS. rule.mitre.id contains T1200. The file was created 10m49s after the initial wireless scan in step 1 — same operator session.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:24:55+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Command and Control / Initial Access — Wi-Fi Networks", "technique": "T1200", "what_happened": "At 09:25:12 CEST (17 seconds after the profile file was written), an 'nmcli connection up' attempt was logged for the rogue profile, targeting SSID 'FreeWiFi_20260512'. Wazuh fired rule 92954 'Connection to unknown SSID' [T1200] at high. The connection attempt itself confirms intent — this is not a stale leftover profile, the host actively tried to associate with the unauthorized open network. An open (unencrypted) SSID that the corporate host autoconnects to is a textbook out-of-band channel / adversary-in-the-middle exposure: any traffic over it can be intercepted, and the SSID could be a personal hotspot, a rogue AP, or an attacker bridge.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92954 fired on NetworkManager 'connection up' / activation event for profile rogue-ap-20260512_091406, SSID FreeWiFi_20260512, agent.name=UBNT-FLOOR2-OPS. rule.mitre.id contains T1200. 17 seconds after the step 3 FIM event — tight operator sequence.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:25:12+0200"}
  ],
